== Chapter V

#align(center)[#block(width: 85%)[
  #set par(justify: false)
  #strong[Showing the Reason for the Proposition.]
]]

#v(0.9em)

#smallcaps[The] great reason why all things will work for good#footnote[#emph[Reason.]] is the near and dear interest that God has in his people. The Lord has made a covenant with them: “They will be My people, and I will be their God” (Jer 32:38). By virtue of this compact all things do, and must, work for good to them. “I am God, your God” (Ps 50:7). This word אלהיךָ, #emph[your God,] is the sweetest word in the Bible. It implies the best relations, and it is impossible that there should be these relations between God and his people and everything not work for their good. This expression, #emph[I am your God,] implies:

1\. The relation of a physician: I am your physician. God is 1. a #emph[skillful] physician, so he knows what is best #footnote[οὐκ οὕτως ἴσμεν τὸ συμφέρον ἡμῖν ὡς αὐτὸς ἐπίσταται. \[We do not know what is good for us as well as he knows it.\] #emph[Chrysost.]]. God observes the different temperaments of men and knows what will work most effectually. Some are of a sweeter disposition and are drawn by mercy; others are more rugged and knotty pieces, and these God deals with in a more forceful way. Some things are preserved in sugar, some in brine. God does not deal alike with all; he has trials for the strong and cordials for the weak. 2. God is a #emph[faithful] physician, so he will turn all to the best. If he does not give to you #emph[ad voluntatem,] it will be #emph[ad sanitatem] #footnote[#emph[Augustine.]]\; if God does not give you what you like, he will give you what you need. A physician does not take such care to please the patient’s taste as to cure his disease. We complain that such sore trials lie on us. Let us remember that God is our physician, so he works to heal us rather than to humor us. God’s dealings with his children, though they are #emph[sharp,] are #emph[safe,] and they aim at a cure: “so that in the end He might do you good” (Deut 8:16).

This word, #emph[your God,] implies the relation of a father. A father loves his child, so whether it is a smile or a stroke, it is for the good of the child. I am #emph[your God,] your Father, so all I do is for your good: “Just as a man chastens his son, so the LORD your God chastens you” (Deut 8:5). God’s chastening is not to destroy but to reform. God cannot hurt his children, for 1. he is a #emph[tender-hearted] Father: “As a father has compassion on his children, so the LORD has compassion on those who fear Him” (Ps 103:13). Will a father seek the ruin of his child, the child who came from his own body and bears his image? All his care and planning is for his child. On whom does he settle the inheritance but on his child? God is tender-hearted, the “Father of mercies” (2 Cor 1:3). He begets all the mercies and tender affections in the creatures. 2. He is an #emph[everlasting] Father (Isa 9:7). He was our Father from eternity: before we were children, God was our Father, and he will be our Father to eternity. A father provides for his child while he lives; but the father dies, and then the child may be exposed to injury. But God never ceases to be a Father. You who are a believer have a Father who never dies, and if God is your Father, you can never be undone: all things must necessarily work for your good.

3\. This word, #emph[your God,] carries the relation of a husband, and this is a near and sweet relation. The husband seeks the good of his spouse; he would be unnatural who set out to destroy his wife: “No one ever hated his own body” (Eph 5:29). There is a marriage relation between God and his people #footnote[#emph[Anima fidelis Deum habet sponsum.] \[The faithful soul has God for its bridegroom.\] Gulielm. Paris.]: “Your husband is your Maker” (Isa 54:5). God loves his people with his whole heart (Isa 43:4). #emph[He engraves them on the palms of his hands] (Isa 49:16). #emph[He sets them as a seal on his heart] (Song 8:6). #emph[He will give kingdoms for their ransom] (Isa 43:3). This shows how near they lie to his heart. If he is a husband whose heart is captured with love, then he will seek the good of his spouse: either he will ward off an injury, or he will turn it to the best.

4\. This word, #emph[your God,] implies the relation of a friend: “This is my friend” (Song 5:16). A friend is #emph[animae dimidium] (as #emph[Augustine] says), half of one’s self. He is eager and inquiring how he may do his friend good; he promotes his friend’s welfare as his own. #emph[Jonathan] risked the king’s displeasure for his friend #emph[David] (1 Sam 19:4). God is our friend, so he will turn all things to our good. There are false friends; Christ was betrayed by a friend. But God is the best friend.

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[1\. He is a faithful friend: “Know therefore that the LORD your God is God, the faithful God” (Deut 7:9). 1. He is faithful in his love. He gave his very heart to us when he gave the Son out of his bosom; here was a pattern of love without a parallel. 2. He is faithful in his promises: “God, who cannot lie, promised” (Titus 1:2). He may change his promise, but he cannot break it. 3. He is faithful in his dealings. When he is afflicting, he is faithful: “In faithfulness You have afflicted me” (Ps 119:75). He is sifting us and refining us like silver (Ps 66:10).]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[2\. He is an unchangeable friend: “Never will I leave you, never will I forsake you” (Heb 13:5). There are five seals set to that promise, οὐ, μὴ, οὐδ’, οὐ, μὴ \[five words for “not”\]. Friends often fail in a pinch. Many deal with their friends as women do with their flowers: while they are fresh they put them in their bosoms, but when they begin to wither they throw them away. Or they deal with them as the traveler does with the sundial. If the sun shines on the dial, the traveler will step out of the road and look at the dial; but if the sun does not shine on it, he will ride by and never take any notice of it. So if prosperity shines on men, their friends will look at them; but if there is a cloud of adversity on them, they will not come near them. But God is a friend forever: #emph[Never will I leave you.] Though #emph[David] walked through the shadow of death, he knew he had a friend beside him: “I will fear no evil, for You are with me” (Ps 23:4). God never wholly takes his love away from his people. It is #emph[amicitia immortalis] #footnote[#emph[Augustine.]] \[an immortal friendship\]: “He loved them ἐις τέλος, to the very end” (John 13:1). God being such a friend, he will make all things work for our good. There is no friend who will not seek the good of his friend.]]

#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[5\. This word, #emph[your God,] carries a still nearer relation, the relation between the head and the members. There is a mystical union between Christ and the saints. He is called κεφαλὴ, #emph[the head of the church] (Eph 5:23). Does not the head take care for the good of the body? The head guides the body and feels with it; it is the fountain of the spirits; it sends out influence and comfort into the body. All the parts of the head are placed for the good of the body. The eye is set, as it were, in the watchtower; it stands sentinel to spy any danger that may come to the body, and to prevent it. The tongue is both a taster and an orator. If the body is a microcosm, or little world, the head is the sun in this world, from which comes the light of reason. The head is placed for the good of the body. Christ and the saints make one mystical body. Our head is in heaven, and surely he will not allow his body to be hurt, but will take care for its safety, and make all things work for the good of the mystical body.]
